numeric constants. Marker names are reserved to the declarations.
A \code{tag} is a registered deterministic JAGS function with a name prefixed
with \verb{<lowercased-module>_} for a provider listed in \code{jags_modules}. It must
return a fixed finite value for admissible arguments. Use a dedicated
function without aliases. The model must reserve that function for tags
to prevent JAGS from reusing a scientific node. The tag creates an
unmonitored terminal node with no observed likelihood contribution.
The BayesTools finite readout functions are reserved for \code{sampler_probabilities}.
A \code{distribution} must be a normalized point mass at zero for every
admissible parent value. Its marker is observed at zero.
Use a distribution name prefixed with
\verb{d<lowercased-module>_} for a provider listed in \code{jags_modules}, or use
the BayesTools block marker \code{dbt_block}. Declare BayesTools blocks through
one of \code{sampler_joint} or \code{sampler_blocks}. Use \code{sampler_gates} for model-verified
gates, which take precedence over \code{dbt_block} when both mark a node.
Supply package and module requirements through \code{required_packages}
and \code{jags_modules}. The
validated declaration is stored in the \code{joint} entry of
\code{attr(fit, "sampler_declarations")}. Extensions reuse the compiled model
text and data.}

\item{sampler_probabilities}{optional character vector of sampled finite
indicator node names or scalar indexed references in the compiled model.
\code{fit$sampler_probabilities} gives one entry per requested indicator.
The recorder can replace an active JAGS 5 \code{base::Finite} update for a
full-rank scalar \code{dbern} or \code{dcat} with fixed integer support starting at
0 or 1, respectively. It preserves competing sampler assignments. Its
entry is an \code{mcmc.list} with one row per retained draw in the same chain
and iteration order as the model draws. The columns \code{p[k]} and
\code{log_p[k]} are conditional probabilities and log probabilities at the
indicator update in that iteration for support label \code{k}. The columns
\code{selected} and \code{lower} give the sampled label and the lowest support
label. Compiled aliases of one node return the same trace. An entry is
\code{NULL} when no recorder was assigned to that node. A probability trace
requires the same availability and support labels
on every worker.
Extensions append the corresponding rows. By default no probabilities
are recorded. An incomplete or incompatible recording raises a
\code{BayesTools_sampler_probabilities_error} backend condition.}

\item{sampler_nested_weights}{logical. Share physical weight coordinates for eligible
nested cumulative one-sided weights in a publication-bias mixture.
The scientific priors and public weights are preserved. Unsupported pairs
and mixtures whose private weight nodes are explicitly used or monitored
keep their ordinary parameterization. The \code{nested_weights} entry in
\code{attr(fit, "sampler_declarations")} records the two branch indices when
sharing was used or the reason it was declined. Defaults to \code{FALSE}.}

\item{...}{additional hidden arguments}

\item{fit}{a 'BayesTools_fit' object (created by \code{JAGS_fit()} function) to be
extended. A derived-draw view signals
\code{BayesTools_draws_view_sampling_unavailable}, also
\code{BayesTools_sampling_unavailable} and
\code{BayesTools_draws_view_unavailable}, before runtime arguments are forced;
extend its \code{original_fit} and regenerate it with \code{\link[=JAGS_with_draws]{JAGS_with_draws()}}.
Fits created by BayesTools 0.4.0 or earlier (runjags fits) and fits
without the parameter map and fit contract of this version of BayesTools
must be refitted: they stop with an error of class
\code{BayesTools_refit_required} (see \code{\link[=JAGS_validate_fit_contract]{JAGS_validate_fit_contract()}}).}
}
\value{
A successful fit is a \code{BayesTools_fit}. It contains the sampled
chains, model and data used for compilation, monitored nodes, and final
per-chain parameter and RNG states. Extract the chains with
\code{coda::as.mcmc.list(fit)}. The sampler table \code{fit$samplers} is from
the most recent successful backend run. For parallel fits it is the first
worker's table. If worker assignments differ, \code{fit$samplers_by_worker}
gives each worker's \code{chains} and \code{samplers}. A failed initial fit returns its error
condition.

\code{JAGS_extend()} rebuilds the sampler from the stored states, adapts
it for \code{adapt * thin} iterations when needed, then appends draws
without further burn-in. A saved and reloaded fit extends from the same state.
Its extensions continue, as automatic fitting in \code{JAGS_fit()}, until
every diagnostic that can be assessed meets the criteria of
\code{autofit_control} after an extension, or until \code{max_time} or
\code{max_extend} stops them. Within one call, automatic extensions
continue the serial sampler. JASP fits
also keep parallel worker samplers through progress updates and automatic
extensions. Other parallel fits rebuild at each extension. The returned fit stores
ordinary R data and can be serialized with \code{saveRDS()}.

The \code{adaptation} attribute of a fit records the adaptation settings
in draws at the fit's thinning (the backend adapts for \code{adapt * thin}
iterations where a sampler adapts): \code{initial}, the \code{adapt} of
\code{JAGS_fit()}, and \code{extensions}, a data frame with one row
per backend extension (automatic extensions of \code{JAGS_fit()} and the
extensions of \code{JAGS_extend()}) giving the \code{adapt} passed to the
backend and whether the backend \code{rebuilt} the sampler, the only case in
which it adapts. Warnings of the backend that adaptation was incomplete
(class \code{BayesTools_adaptation_incomplete}, also
\code{BayesTools_fit_diagnostic}) are issued and also kept in the fit's
\code{warnings} attribute.

The \code{burnin_check} attribute of a fit records the readiness check
between burn-in and sampling (see \code{burnin_max_Rhat} in
\code{autofit_control}); it is \code{NULL} when the check did not run. It
runs in the initial fit of \code{JAGS_fit()} only, afresh in each restart
attempt, and not in \code{JAGS_extend()}, the automatic extensions after
sampling, or a model without samplers. The record holds
\code{burnin_max_Rhat} (the threshold used, a number also when it was
\code{"auto"}), \code{block} (the draws of the checked end of the
requested burn-in), \code{burnin_extend}, \code{thin} (the thinning of the
checked draws), \code{blocks} (one row per checked block, with its
\code{draws} and its largest value \code{Rhat} and \code{parameter}),
\code{extension} (the draws added to burn-in),
\code{status} (\code{"ready"}, \code{"not_ready"}, or
\code{"not_assessable"} when no quantity could be assessed, which makes no
claim of readiness and extends nothing), \code{stop_reason}
(\code{"ready"}, \code{"max_extend"}, \code{"max_time"},
\code{"iteration_limit"}, \code{"no_autofit"}, or \code{"not_assessable"}),
and \code{failing} (the
quantities of the last block above the threshold, with their values). Its
counts are draws at the fit's thinning, as are \code{fit$sample} and
\code{fit$burnin}, the burn-in that was run; each draw stands for
\code{thin} iterations, which the iteration numbers of the draws and of
\code{sampler_probabilities} count.
}
\description{
Compiles JAGS model syntax, data, formulas and priors into
a model and samples it using \pkg{rjags}.
}
\examples{
\dontrun{
# simulate data
set.seed(1)
data <- list(
  x = rnorm(10),
  N = 10
)
data$x

# define priors
priors_list <- list(mu = prior("normal", list(0, 1)))

# define likelihood for the data
model_syntax <-
  "model{
    for(i in 1:N){
      x[i] ~ dnorm(mu, 1)
    }
  }"

# fit the models
fit <- JAGS_fit(model_syntax, data, priors_list)
}

}
\seealso{
\code{\link[=JAGS_check_convergence]{JAGS_check_convergence()}}
}
